        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This should now be resolved on latest `main` with addition of two new groups: [`MiniDiffOverChangeBuf`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/4630b9cb783b280fa0fd94e8245d8fb06023f3ba/doc/mini-diff.txt#L68) and [`MiniDiffOverContextBuf`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/4630b9cb783b280fa0fd94e8245d8fb06023f3ba/doc/mini-diff.txt#L70). This allows having GitHub-style diff highlighting with something like this:\n\n```lua\nlocal hi = function(name, bg) vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, name, { bg = bg }) end\nhi('MiniDiffOverAdd', '#104010') -- regular green\nhi('MiniDiffOverChange', '#600000') -- saturated red\nhi('MiniDiffOverChangeBuf', '#006000') -- saturated green\nhi('MiniDiffOverContext', '#401010') -- regular red\nhi('MiniDiffOverContextBuf', '#104010') -- regular green\nhi('MiniDiffOverDelete', '#401010') -- regular red\n```\n\nThis is indeed not 100% solution, as with `config.options.linematch = 0` change hunks with unequal amount of reference and buffer lines will have reference changes highlighted with saturated red (and not regular red). The buffer part will be highlighted as regular green as `MiniDiffOverContextBuf` is used in this case (and not `MiniDiffOverChangeBuf`mostly to avoid the situation when both reference and buffer parts are highlighted the same; as is with default highlight groups). But I think in practice it is at least a 95% solution, as I don't expect the situation of unequal number of lines in change hunks to be that common. It should be good enough.",
